he good directory's output, so I can't use a blanket discard approach. Let me just check what error messages each tool actually produces for a nonexistent path to see how different they really are.\n\nThe formats are completely different between GNU gdu and the Go version, so I need a strategy that either discards stderr entirely, extracts just the path from error messages, or falls back to manual testing. Since the NormalizeFunc API can't distinguish between different streams, I can't selectively zero stderr while preserving stdout. I'll go with differential tests for the valid path cases and manual tests for error handling to verify exit codes, stderr content, and the continue-processing behavior.\n\nFor the mixed arguments case where one path is bad and one is good, I need to check that the Go binary exits with code 1, outputs stderr about the bad path, and still prints stdout sizes for the good path. I could compare against gdu's output for just the good path alone, or try comparing both binaries' stdout with the same mixed arguments—but that still requires normalizing stderr, which DiffTest doesn't support. I'll stick with manual tests for R4.2 and also add a manual test for the SIGPIPE case following the pattern from cat_test.go. I'm realizing the normalizer function can't distinguish between stdout and stderr since it just receives raw bytes, so I can't selectively discard only stderr. For the \"nonexistent\" case, I'll use a normalizer that clears all output to nil since stdout is empty anyway, and for the multi-argument case I'll need a separate manual test to verify stdout while ignoring the stderr differences.\n\nNow I'm stepping back to clarify what I actually need to test: R4.1 requires explicit tests for exit 0 on both basic and summary modes, while R4.2 needs three separate validations—exit code 1, stderr diagnostics, and continued processing across multiple arguments. A manual test handles the first two nicely, but for the third I need to verify that